.\" Man page for ax25netd
.\"
.TH AX25NETD 8 "7 Aug 2026" "ax25-apps" "Linux System Administration"
.SH NAME
ax25netd \- AGWPE multiplexing daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ax25netd
[\-f] [\-d] [\-c \fIfile\fR] [\-C \fIfile\fR] [\-b \fIaddr\fR] [\-p \fIport\fR]
[\-U \fIsocket\fR] [\-g \fIgroup\fR] [\-\-no-tcp] [\-u \fIuser\fR] [\-\-no-mheard]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ax25netd
multiplexes one or more AGWPE servers ("upstreams") behind a single local
AGWPE port.  AGWPE client programs connect to the loop port and their
commands are forwarded to the configured upstreams; frames coming back are
delivered to the client which registered the addressed call sign.
.PP
The reserved virtual "loop" upstream (AGWPE port 255) has no radio behind
it: instead it bridges frames between local clients, so a local AX.25
service (ax25d, axspawn, ...) that binds a call sign on the loop port is
reachable like a station on a radio.  See
.BR axports (5)
for the "loop" port entry.
.PP
The kernel AX.25 stack of libax25 is not involved.  On Linux the native
kernel AX.25 support continues to work exactly as before.
.PP
For connects that name no digipeaters the daemon mirrors the kernel
AX.25 stack: it asks the
.BR ax25rtd (8)
route cache for a learned path to the destination on the target port
and uses it (see
.B autoroute
in
.BR agwpe.conf (5)).
This is off for the virtual loop port, and connects with an explicit
digipeater path are never resolved.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-f
Stay in the foreground (do not daemonize).
.TP
.B \-d
Also log to standard error.
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Configuration file, default \fI@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/agwpe.conf\fR.
.TP
.BI \-C " file"
Shared loop port configuration, default
\fI@@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf\fR; also read by
.BR ax25tcpd (8).
A missing file leaves the defaults in place (see
.BR ax25common.conf (5)).
.TP
.BI \-b " addr"
Address the loop port binds to; IPv4 and IPv6 addresses (e.g.
.I 192.168.1.10
or
.IR ::1 )
are supported.  Default 127.0.0.1.
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
TCP port of the loop port, default 8100.
.TP
.BI \-U " socket"
Also listen on this unix domain socket.  Overrides the
.B loop socket
directive of
.BR ax25common.conf (5).
.TP
.BI \-g " group"
Group allowed to connect to the unix socket: a group name, a numeric
gid, or
.B all
for every local user.  Overrides the
.B loop group
directive of
.BR ax25common.conf (5).
.TP
.B \-\-no-tcp
Do not listen on TCP at all; the unix socket becomes the only way in.
Requires
.B \-U
or a
.B loop socket
in
.BR ax25common.conf (5).
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
If started as root, drop to this user after the configuration is read and
the sockets are set up.  The daemon never needs root, so it is safe to run
it under a dedicated unprivileged account.
.TP
.B \-\-no-mheard
Do not maintain the heard-station list (see
.B MHEARD
below).  Use this on storage-constrained embedded systems or when
.B mheardd (8)
already runs on the same file.
.SH MHEARD
The radio traffic passing through
.B ax25netd
is kept in a heard-station list, the same
.I mheard.dat
file that
.BR mheardd (8)
maintains on a native Linux AX.25 system, so that
.BR mheard (1)
reports stations heard through the AGWPE path as well.  The file is
\fI@@@AX25_LOCALSTATEDIR@@@/mheard/mheard.dat\fR.
.PP
The list is fed from the raw frames the upstream delivers ('K') and from
outgoing frames of the loop clients.  For the raw frames to be available the
upstream keeps its raw monitoring enabled as long as the list is on, even
when no client asked for raw frames.
.PP
Every station's record is written at most once a minute and new stations on
their first hear; a per-frame write would otherwise wear out flash storage
on embedded systems.  The records of a crashed or killed daemon are
therefore at most a minute old.
.PP
A system that runs the native kernel stack and
.B mheardd
at the same time updates the same file; both daemons serialize their writes
with a file lock.
.SH SECURITY
By default the loop port listens on loopback only and is therefore reachable
only from the host itself.  Binding to any other address hands control of
your radio to every caller that can reach that address.  Use client
authentication when listening beyond loopback: enable
.B auth
in
.BR agwpe.conf (5)
and create credentials in
.BR agwpe_shadow.conf (5).
ax25netd warns at startup when it listens on a non-loopback address without
authentication.
.PP
The daemon does not require root.  It only reads the configuration, binds
the loop port and connects outbound to the upstreams.  Run it as an
unprivileged user (see
.B \-u
above); that account needs read access to the configuration and the shadow
file.
.SH CHAINS
Because upstreams are ordinary AGWPE servers, several ax25netd instances
can be stacked: a remote box lists a "master" box as an upstream and the
master authenticates it with its own
.B ax25netd
credentials from
.BR agwpe_shadow.conf (5).
This aggregates the radios of several hosts behind one master AGWPE port.
.PP
Upstreams need not be TCP: an upstream on the same host can be reached over
a unix domain socket (a
.I host
starting with
.B /
in
.BR agwpe.conf (5)),
and IPv6 addresses are supported in the bracketed
.B [2001:db8::1]
notation.
Outbound connects are non-blocking and are retried every few seconds while
an upstream is down.
.SH PORT NUMBERS
The AGWPE port numbers seen by the loop clients are flat and depend only on
the order of the upstreams in
.BR agwpe.conf (5),
never on the channel layout of the upstreams: upstream \fIi\fR (counting
from 0) owns the ports \fIi*16\fR .. \fIi*16+15\fR and its channel \fIc\fR
is port \fIi*16+c\fR.  A single-channel Direwolf therefore keeps port
number 0.  The port list is learned from each upstream's reply to the
"port information" query, so the numbering survives an upstream restart
with a changed number of channels.
.PP
A "port information" ('G') request is held until every connected upstream
has reported its port list, so a client that connects right after a
startup or an upstream reconnect never sees a partial list; the request is
answered with what is known after at most
.B 10
seconds.
.PP
The channel number carried in the header of every frame is only meaningful
relative to its upstream.  The loop port is the interface the client
addresses; the multiplexer translates port to channel when forwarding a
frame to an upstream and back when delivering a frame to a client, so
applications never have to deal with upstream channel numbers.
.SH AX.25 LEVEL 2
.B ax25netd
speaks only the AGWPE protocol and does not implement AX.25 level 2.  Link
establishment (SABM vs.\ SABME), sequence numbering (modulus 8 vs.\ 128) and
retransmissions are performed by the upstream AGWPE server, e.g.\ Direwolf.
.PP
The AGWPE protocol has no field for the AX.25 mode, so the mode requested by a
client (the
.B \-m
option of
.BR call (1),
i.e.\ the
.B AX25_EXTSEQ
socket option) is not forwarded and has no effect on connections made through
.B ax25netd.
It remains available only to applications that use native kernel AX.25
sockets directly.
.PP
Like DAMA, which is a property of the radio interface (KISS) configuration
rather than of the client, the level 2 mode is configured on the upstream:
in
.B direwolf.conf
via
.B MAXV22
and
.B V20
(see the Direwolf User Guide).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/agwpe.conf
Upstream and authentication configuration.
.TP
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/ax25common.conf
Loop port endpoint, shared with the local clients
.RB ( ax25tcpd (8),
AGWPE shim).
.TP
.I @@@AX25_SYSCONFDIR@@@/agwpe_shadow.conf
Credentials (mode 0600, owned by root).
.TP
.I @@@AX25_LOCALSTATEDIR@@@/mheard/mheard.dat
Heard-station list (see
.BR MHEARD ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR agwpe.conf (5),
.BR ax25common.conf (5),
.BR agwpe_shadow.conf (5),
.BR axports (5),
.BR ax25tcpd (8),
.BR ax25rtd (8),
.BR call (1),
.BR mheard (1),
.BR mheardd (8)
